BODY = '''<h2>What the CDC Says About the Bathroom</h2>
<p>The CDC says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, that fewer than half tell their doctor, and that about 37% of people who fall report an injury that needed medical treatment or limited their activity for at least a day. A single fall doubles the chance of another.</p>
<p>The CDC's "Check for Safety" home checklist covers the bathroom in two lines. If the tub or shower floor is slippery, it suggests a non-slip rubber mat or self-stick strips. If you need support getting in and out of the tub or up from the toilet, it suggests grab bars next to and inside the tub and next to the toilet. It does not mention raised seats. MedlinePlus, in its "Bathroom safety for adults" page (reviewed April 2026), says raising the toilet seat height can help prevent falls.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>A raised seat shortens the distance, and a grab bar gives you something to hold.</strong> The picks with handles help with pushing up, but they are not fixed to the wall. If standing from the toilet is hard, ask a doctor, physical therapist or occupational therapist to look at the whole bathroom, and use the CDC checklist to find the other hazards.</p>

<h2>How to Measure for a Raised Toilet Seat</h2>
<p>The Joint Replacement Resource Centre, in a handout hosted by the Winnipeg Regional Health Authority, gives a method that needs a tape measure and a chair. Its aim is to sit with your feet on the floor and your hips about 2 inches higher than your knees. If your knees are higher than your hips, it says, it is much harder to get off the toilet.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Measure the toilet.</strong> Measure from the floor to the top of the bowl, without the seat or lid.</li>
<li><strong>Find your ideal seated height.</strong> Sit on a bed or chair where your feet are flat and your hips are about 2 inches above your knees, in the shoes you wear, and measure from the floor to the seat.</li>
<li><strong>Subtract.</strong> Your seat height is the ideal total minus the toilet height. The handout's example is a 19-inch ideal height on a 15-inch toilet, which means a 4-inch raised seat.</li>
<li><strong>Note the bowl shape.</strong> Check whether your bowl is round or elongated. The handout also says that if your hips and knees are level when you sit, a 2 to 3 inch seat may work.</li>
</ol>
<p>Check the height the maker lists against your number. The five picks here add 3, 3, 3.5, 4 and 5 inches.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Raised Toilet Seat</h2>
<h3>Height</h3>
<p>Use the measurement above. A seat that is too low does little, and one that is too high leaves your feet off the floor. If your number falls between two sizes, choose the lower one and recheck it with your shoes on.</p>
<h3>Round or elongated</h3>
<p>Round bowls are shorter from front to back, and elongated bowls are longer. A seat made for one will not sit right on the other. Three of the five here are for elongated bowls and two are for round, and Vive sells both shapes in its range.</p>
<h3>Arms or no arms</h3>
<p>Arms give you something to push on, and they take room. Vive and Carex have removable padded arms. The Bemis, AquaSense and HealthSmart have none.</p>
<h3>How it attaches</h3>
<p>The Bemis bolts to the bowl. The Vive goes under your seat with screws and washers. The Carex, AquaSense and HealthSmart sit under your existing seat, per their listings. After you fit any of them, sit and stand a few times and check that nothing slides or rocks before you use it alone.</p>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>The limits run from 250 pounds on the Carex to 400 pounds on the AquaSense, and the Bemis lists a tested load of 1,000 pounds. Check the limit against the heaviest person who will use it.</p>

<h2>Raised Toilet Seat Questions</h2>
<h3>How high should a raised toilet seat be?</h3>
<p>The Joint Replacement Resource Centre says to aim for feet flat on the floor and hips about 2 inches above your knees. Measure your toilet and your ideal seated height, then subtract. If your hips and knees are level, the handout says a 2 to 3 inch seat may work.</p>
<h3>Will it fit my toilet?</h3>
<p>Only if the shape matches. Check whether your bowl is round or elongated, and compare it with the title of each listing. Vive says its riser fits a toilet with an interior lip.</p>
<h3>Is a raised seat the same as a grab bar?</h3>
<p>No. The CDC's checklist suggests grab bars next to the toilet for support, and does not mention raised seats. Handles on a raised seat help with pushing up, but they are not fixed to the wall.</p>
<h3>Do the handles come off?</h3>
<p>On the Vive and the Carex they do, according to the maker and the listing, and both are padded. Vive says its handles are 19 inches apart on the elongated size. Take them off if someone does not want them or if the toilet is in a narrow space, but remember that the handles give you something to push on, so keep them on for anyone who is unsteady standing up.</p>
<h3>Do I keep my old toilet seat?</h3>
<p>It depends on the model. The Carex listing says the toilet seat and lid are not included and that you use your current ones with the riser, the Vive installs under your existing seat with screws and washers, and the HealthSmart listing says to lift your seat, set the riser in place and lower the seat. The Bemis Assurance is a complete seat that bolts to the bowl, so it replaces your old one.</p>
<h3>How do I tell whether my bowl is round or elongated?</h3>
<p>Look at the bowl from above. A round bowl is shorter from front to back and almost circular, and an elongated bowl is longer and oval. The Joint Replacement Resource Centre says to note the shape before you buy, and the listings here name the shape: the Bemis, Vive and Carex are for elongated bowls, and the AquaSense and HealthSmart for round. Vive sells both shapes.</p>

<h2>What the Research Says</h2>
<p>MedlinePlus says raising the toilet seat height can help prevent falls, but we found no trial of raised seats alone. Home safety programs that included changes like this reduced falls in a Cochrane review of 159 trials, as <a href="/articles/do-home-safety-changes-prevent-falls/">our summary</a> explains.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Bemis Assurance is the best pick if you can live with a 3-inch rise. It bolts to the bowl, the listing says it is tested to 1,000 pounds, and it has the best rating of the five at 4.6 from 1,405 reviews. The cost is the price and the bolting, and we could not read Bemis's own page, so check its height and bowl shape on the listing before you order.</p>
<p>If you want something to push on, the choice is between the Vive and the Carex. The extra cost buys a 300-pound limit against 250, a maker page with full measurements and a lifetime guarantee. Both are for elongated bowls and both have removable arms. If everyone who will use it weighs well under 250 pounds, the Carex is the better value.</p>
<p>The two plain risers suit round bowls. The AquaSense is the lightest and has the highest limit, 400 pounds on Drive's page, so it is the one to take to a visit. The HealthSmart adds 5 inches, which some people need, but that is too much for others. Measure first, and let the number choose between the 4-inch and the 5-inch.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Bemis Assurance is the best all-round raised toilet seat for an elongated bowl, with the Vive and Carex as the choices if you want arms. For a round bowl, the AquaSense is the portable pick and the HealthSmart is the tallest. Measure your toilet and your seated height before you buy, and add a grab bar next to the toilet if you can. See also our guides to <a href="/best-shower-chairs/">shower chairs</a>, <a href="/best-bedside-commodes/">bedside commodes</a> and <a href="/best-rollators/">rollators</a>.</p>'''

